d index on the CustomerName column. This can be done by creating an index on the UPPER(CustomerName) column, like this:\n```sql\nCREATE INDEX my_index ON myTable (UPPER(CustomerName))\n```\nThis rewritten query should also be faster since the function-based index is more efficient than the LIKE operator."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 8:\n"""We can also try rewriting the query by using a full-text search instead of the LIKE operator. This can be done by creating a full-text index on the CustomerName column and then using the CONTAINS function to search for the string, like this:\n```sql\nSELECT * \nFROM myTable \nWHERE CONTAINS(UPPER(CustomerName), \'ABC\')\n```\nThis rewritten query should also be faster since the full-text search is more efficient than the LIKE operator."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 9:\n"""Rewrite the query by using a regular expression instead of the LIKE operator. This can be done by using the REGEXP_LIKE function in Oracle, like this:\n```sql\nSELECT * \nFROM myTable \nWHERE REGEXP_LIKE(UPPER(CustomerName), \'^ABC%\')\n```\nThis rewritten query should be faster since the regular expression is more efficient than the LIKE operator."""'}], 'model': 'llama3:8b', 'max_tokens': 1024, 'stream': False, 'temperature': 0.1}}
