17:49:47,236 root DEBUG {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You will be given a SQL query and some strategies to rewrite the given SQL query. Your task is to summarize the provided query rewrite strategies into one paragraph.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '\nSQL Query:\n```sql\nSELECT MIN(kt.kind) AS movie_kind,\n       MIN(t.title) AS complete_nerdy_internet_movie\nFROM complete_cast AS cc,\n     comp_cast_type AS cct1,\n     company_name AS cn,\n     company_type AS ct,\n     info_type AS it1,\n     keyword AS k,\n     kind_type AS kt,\n     movie_companies AS mc,\n     movie_info AS mi,\n     movie_keyword AS mk,\n     title AS t\nWHERE cct1.kind = \'complete+verified\'\n  AND cn.country_code = \'[us]\'\n  AND it1.info = \'release dates\'\n  AND k.keyword IN (\'nerd\',\n                    \'loner\',\n                    \'alienation\',\n                    \'dignity\')\n  AND kt.kind IN (\'movie\')\n  AND mi.note LIKE \'%internet%\'\n  AND mi.info LIKE \'USA:% 200%\'\n  AND t.production_year > 2000\n  AND kt.id = t.kind_id\n  AND t.id = mi.movie_id\n  AND t.id = mk.movie_id\n  AND t.id = mc.movie_id\n  AND t.id = cc.movie_id\n  AND mk.movie_id = mi.movie_id\n  AND mk.movie_id = mc.movie_id\n  AND mk.movie_id = cc.movie_id\n  AND mi.movie_id = mc.movie_id\n  AND mi.movie_id = cc.movie_id\n  AND mc.movie_id = cc.movie_id\n  AND k.id = mk.keyword_id\n  AND it1.id = mi.info_type_id\n  AND cn.id = mc.company_id\n  AND ct.id = mc.company_type_id\n  AND cct1.id = cc.status_id;\n```\n\nQuery Rewrite Strategies:\nQuery Rewrite 1:\n"""According to the query rewrite rule, when Case 1 is matched, the transformations are as follows:\n\n- Directly apply the `GROUP BY` and aggregate functions to the data source referred to in the `FROM` clause of the inner query before the projection.\n- Eliminate the outer projection layer if it only renames the columns without transforming them.\n\nIn our case, the rewritten query applies the `MIN` aggregate function directly to the `kind` and `title` columns involved in the `GROUP BY` clause. This is done by applying the aggregate functions to the data source referred to in the `FROM` clause of the inner query before the projection. The outer projection layer only renames the columns without transforming them, so it can be eliminated.\n\nTherefore, the SQL query rewrite rule applies to the given SQL query by directly applying the `GROUP BY` and aggregate functions to the data source referred to in the `FROM` clause of the inner query before the projection, and eliminating the outer projection layer if it only renames the columns without transforming them."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 2:\n"""The query rewrite rule\'s Case 3 applies to this specific query. This means that the rewritten query has been transformed into a more efficient form by converting certain OUTER JOINS to INNER JOINS. The detailed changes in the SQL query support this conclusion:\n\n* The original query had multiple OUTER JOINS with conditions that effectively mandated the presence of non-NULL values in certain columns. These conditions have been moved into the WHERE clause, which has converted the OUTER JOINS to INNER JOINS.\n* The rewritten query now uses INNER JOINS instead of OUTER JOINS, which can improve performance by reducing the number of rows being processed.\n\nIn summary, the query rewrite rule\'s Case 3 applies to this specific query. The rewritten query has been transformed into a more efficient form by converting certain OUTER JOINS to INNER JOINS, which can improve performance and reduce the number of rows being processed."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 3:\n"""Rewrite the query by executing the subquery separately and then using the result set in the outer query. This can be done by replacing the IN subquery with a static list of values, like this:\n```sql\nSELECT \n    distinct \n    u.uuid,\n    u.user_name,\n    u.key\nFROM request req\nJOIN int_user u ON u.uuid = req.user_uuid\nJOIN int_right r ON r.uuid = req.right_uuid\nWHERE r.uuid IN (\'value1\', \'value2\', \'value3\')\n```\nThis rewritten query should be faster since the subquery is executed separately and then used in the outer query."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 4:\n"""Another approach is to use a join instead of an IN subquery. This can be done by joining the int_right table with the request table on the uuid column, like this:\n```sql\nSELECT \n    distinct \n    u.uuid,\n    u.user_name,\n    u.key\nFROM request req\nJOIN int_user u ON u.uuid = req.user_uuid\nJOIN int_right r ON r.uuid = req.right_uuid AND r.uuid IN (\'value1\', \'value2\', \'value3\')\n```\nThis rewritten query should also be faster since the join is more efficient than the IN subquery."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 5:\n"""We can also try rewriting the query by using a correlated subquery instead of an IN subquery. This can be done by joining the int_right table with the request table on the uuid column and then using a correlated subquery to filter the results, like this:\n```sql\nSELECT \n    distinct \n    u.uuid,\n    u.user_name,\n    u.key\nFROM request req\nJOIN int_user u ON u.uuid = req.user_uuid\nWHERE EXISTS (\n    SELECT 1 FROM int_right r WHERE r.uuid = req.right_uuid AND r.uuid IN (\'value1\', \'value2\', \'value3\')\n)\n```\nThis rewritten query should also be faster since the correlated subquery is more efficient than the IN subquery."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 6:\n"""Rewrite the query by using a regular expression instead of the LIKE operator. This can be done by using the REGEXP_LIKE function in Oracle, like this:\n```sql\nSELECT * \nFROM myTable \nWHERE REGEXP_LIKE(UPPER(CustomerName), \'^ABC%\')\n```\nThis rewritten query should be faster since the regular expression is more efficient than the LIKE operator."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 7:\n"""Another approach is to use a function-based index on the CustomerName column. This can be done by creating an index on the UPPER(CustomerName) column, like this:\n```sql\nCREATE INDEX my_index ON myTable (UPPER(CustomerName))\n```\nThis rewritten query should also be faster since the function-based index is more efficient than the LIKE operator."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 8:\n"""We can also try rewriting the query by using a full-text search instead of the LIKE operator. This can be done by creating a full-text index on the CustomerName column and then using the CONTAINS function to search for the string, like this:\n```sql\nSELECT * \nFROM myTable \nWHERE CONTAINS(UPPER(CustomerName), \'ABC\')\n```\nThis rewritten query should also be faster since the full-text search is more efficient than the LIKE operator."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 9:\n"""Rewrite the query by using a regular expression instead of the LIKE operator. This can be done by using the REGEXP_LIKE function in Oracle, like this:\n```sql\nSELECT * \nFROM myTable \nWHERE REGEXP_LIKE(UPPER(CustomerName), \'^ABC%\')\n```\nThis rewritten query should be faster since the regular expression is more efficient than the LIKE operator."""'}], 'response': "Here's a summary of the provided SQL query rewrite strategies in one paragraph:\n\nThe given SQL query can be rewritten using various strategies. According to Query Rewrite 1, when the query matches Case 1, the `GROUP BY` and aggregate functions should be directly applied to the data source before projection, eliminating the outer projection layer if it only renames columns without transforming them. For Query Rewrite 2, the original OUTER JOINS can be converted to INNER JOINS by moving conditions into the WHERE clause, improving performance. Query Rewrites 3-5 suggest replacing IN subqueries with static lists of values, joins, or correlated subqueries for improved efficiency. Additionally, Query Rewrites 6-9 propose rewriting queries using regular expressions, function-based indexes, full-text searches, and REGEXP_LIKE functions to optimize query performance.", 'time': 1.9397599697113037}
